  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 323 PM EDT SUN AUG 23 2026\n\nThe long term period begins Tuesday with a subtropical ridge and\nclosed 500 mb high ranging from 594-597 dm centered over the\nFour Corners region. Elsewhere, an upper-level trough is\nmodeled across the Great Lakes, Mid-Atlantic, and New England.\nAcross eastern Kentucky, Tuesday will be fair and quiet with\ndew points in the 50s, and temperatures in the mid 80s feeling\na bit less humid compared to previous days. Overnight, light\nwinds and clear skies may aid in patchy valley fog developing\nin typical favored river valley locations. With valleys possibly\ndecoupling at night, temperatures in the valleys may cool into\nthe mid to upper 50s, while ridgetops cool into the upper 50s\nto low 60s, Tuesday night.\n\nAn upper low modeled over southern Manitoba will progress\nsoutheast overnight, ending up in the Upper Great Lakes by\nWednesday morning. A surface low will also form in the Great\nLakes vicinity. Some high and mid-level clouds may work into the\narea through Wednesday in advance of the approaching system. By\nWednesday evening, as a surface cold front extends from the\nGreat Lakes southwestward through the Ohio Valley and Ozarks,\nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will develop\nWednesday night into Thursday, peaking Thursday ahead of the\nfrontal passage.\n\nBy Friday, the cold front is expected to have passed east of the\nCommonwealth, with the trough axis shifting east of the forecast\narea Friday into Saturday, leaving broad troughing across the\neastern US. Lingering moisture along the departing trough may\nsupport isolated shower and storm chances Friday afternoon along\nthe VA/KY border. Model and ensemble agreement is poor by next\nweekend. However, deterministic guidance diverges, with one\nscenario depicting a shortwave progressing through the Central\nPlains into portions of the Mid-Mississippi and lower Ohio Valleys\nby Saturday evening into Sunday, which would lead to another round\nof showers and storms across the area on Sunday. Conversely, an\nalternate scenario tracks the shortwave farther north into the\nOhio Valley. NBM has maintained low-end isolated PoPs for Sunday\n(15-20%) to reflect this low-confidence scenario. Temperatures\nshould remain slightly above normal for this time of year, in the\nmid to upper 80s. The CPC 6-10 day outlook supports this thinking.",
